WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.920
MARK MIRCHANDANI: When working
with a job search service,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.378
artificial intelligence
can improve

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:06.120
the experience of your
candidates and employers.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.620
In the last set of
videos, we looked

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.750
at what the Google Cloud
Talent Solution Job Search

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.390
API offers and gave some
general tips for using it.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.079
Now, let's write some code
and start working with the API

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:16.600
itself.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.456
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:22.800
In order to use
the API, we'll need

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.390
to go through code
using the client

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.460
libraries, REST interface,
or RPC interface.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.980
The client libraries are the
easiest way to get started,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.430
so we'll be using those
for any examples here.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.180
But the structure
is still the same

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.660
if you want to use the
REST or RPC interfaces.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.390
We'll be using version three
of the API for all of our code.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.640
Don't forget to check the
documentation for the latest

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.618
updates.

00:00:42.618 --> 00:00:44.910
First thing's first, we'll
need a Google Cloud Platform

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.073
project with billing
enabled to work from.

00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:48.490
If you're not sure
how to do this,

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.970
I'd recommend checking out the
links in the description first,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.770
then coming back to
this video when you've

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.750
got the GCP basics set up.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.505
With the GCP project,
we can open up

00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:58.320
the navigation in the
top left, and look

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.180
for Talent Solution,
which will be

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.190
under Artificial Intelligence.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.750
When we click on
Overview, we should

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.250
get redirected to
a screen that asks

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.365
us to enable the API since this
might be a new GCP project.

00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:11.490
Easily enough, we can just
click that Enable button

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.100
to turn on the API for
the current project.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.260
This may take a few minutes,
but once it's enabled,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.130
anything we do in the context
of this project can use the API.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.380
Keep in mind that if you
want to use multiple projects

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.250
with the API, you'll need to
enable it for each project.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.620
After that's done, we'll head
back to the Overview page

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.240
and have a new screen show up--

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.720
a data-logging
requirement notification.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.220
This is really just making sure
you know that the Job Search

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.220
API is powered by a
machine-learning model that

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.275
needs data.

00:01:39.275 --> 00:01:40.650
As part of the
integration, we'll

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.450
actually be able
to send over events

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.930
from users who are searching
to further train those models.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.580
When this is implemented
through code,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.030
you can specify which pieces
of information get sent over.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.920
We'll dive deeper into
what those events are

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.650
and how to send them later,
but the pre-trained models

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.250
will work just fine for now.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.430
So we'll go ahead and
enable Data Logging

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.070
and click on Service
Account Connections.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.230
See, when making
API requests, you'll

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.750
need to make them on behalf
of an actual authenticated

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.840
account.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.900
Google Cloud Platform's
best practices

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.060
recommend setting up a
service account for this.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.310
Once again, there's a link
in the description for more

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.220
about service accounts, but
think of them as an easy way

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.250
to set up authenticated users
who have limited permissions

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.640
so you can build an
independent and secure system.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.350
We'll need a service account
to use the Job Search API,

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.180
so let's create one and give
it read-write permissions using

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.810
Job Editor under the Cloud
Talent Solution section.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.790
It's also possible to
set up a service account

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.170
with just Job Viewer so it would
only have read-only access.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.090
One more note to be
aware of-- especially

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.160
if you're importing
a lot of jobs at once

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.890
or dealing with a high
volume of search requests,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.600
you should be aware of quotas.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.360
You can see your quotas on
the admin page and request

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.780
increases if needed.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.420
If you see an error message
related to quota or to jobs

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.510
still being indexed,
the quota page

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.188
may help to see what's going on.

00:02:59.188 --> 00:03:01.230
Check out the quota page
in the ever-growing link

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.020
farm down below to see
what the default quotas are

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.400
and how to raise them.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.000
OK.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.260
That's enough set up for now.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.760
We've got everything we
actually need to run some code,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:10.800
so let's do it.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.690
We'll use the Client
Libraries in Python,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.790
but you can check the
docs for code samples

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.170
in your preferred language.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.060
We'll also use the
convenient Cloud Shell

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.050
that you can access
through the console.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.008
If you're doing this from
your own environment,

00:03:22.008 --> 00:03:24.540
make sure to get the Google
Cloud SDK and the client

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.100
libraries installed.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.900
To run the code
using the libraries,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.275
you just need to make sure
some environment variables are

00:03:30.275 --> 00:03:30.860
set up--

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.600
one for the project, and one
for the path to your service

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.705
account's key file.

00:03:34.705 --> 00:03:36.330
Let's start off with
something simple--

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.460
just listening all the
companies that we have.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.350
This is our code on the top and
the Cloud Shell on the bottom.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.933
You can see that we
set up the project

00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:44.400
ID using the
environment variable,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.360
and in the list
companies function,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.020
we'll just list the companies
by passing the project ID.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.070
Then, we'll loop to the
results and print them all out.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.820
Of course, there's no
companies because we

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.390
haven't created any yet.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.350
So let's take a look at how
to create a basic company.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.020
This function will create
a company that's passed in.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.030
The request is pretty
similar to the last one,

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.820
but we're calling create
with some additional fields.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.380
We'll pass in a
very basic company

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.250
with only the required fields--
display name and external ID.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.020
When we run this, we'll see
that the company was created,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.100
and it has a name, which
is a unique identifier used

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.450
in the API.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.070
Now, when we list
our companies, we'll

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.520
see the new one we created,
as well as its name.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.380
See, the name is the
unique identifier

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.040
generated by the Job Search
API, while the external ID is

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.190
the identifier you provide
from your own job system.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.780
Anytime you refer to an
object through the API,

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.325
you'll need to use the name.

00:04:36.325 --> 00:04:37.950
We'll talk about that
in the next video

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.110
when we dive more into
working with companies.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.660
In the meantime, you can always
check out the documentation

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.710
for more info.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.180
Thanks for watching,
and remember,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.847
when looking for talent, it's OK
to keep your head in the Cloud.

00:04:47.847 --> 00:04:50.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

